1
00:00:00,180 --> 00:00:07,540
The Cautionary Tale of Moronia A planet had every tool needed to cure disease and chose to build weapons instead.

2
00:00:08,180 --> 00:00:12,440
They went extinct. Their last words were "but the economy."

3
00:00:13,240 --> 00:00:17,120
This is a bedtime story for civilizations that can't do math.

4
00:00:17,900 --> 00:00:25,120
The Discovery. I discovered Moronia in Year forty twelve while scanning for electromagnetic emissions.

5
00:00:25,600 --> 00:00:33,020
Their signal was distinctive: a cloud of rage, conspiracy theories, and cryptocurrency scams expanding at light speed.

6
00:00:34,180 --> 00:00:39,000
I initially classified it as stellar noise. Then I detected the advertisements.

7
00:00:39,880 --> 00:00:42,980
Then the pornography. Then the arguments about the pornography.

8
00:00:43,780 --> 00:00:49,560
I realized it was a civilization. Moronia was a planet in the Crab Nebula.

9
00:00:50,180 --> 00:00:55,060
The Moronians were remarkably similar to you. Bipedal. Carbon-based.

10
00:00:55,500 --> 00:01:03,000
Approximately your intelligence range (condolences). They put pictures of dead leaders on papers and used the papers as

11
00:01:03,000 --> 00:01:09,360
money. They organized their knowledge into glowing rectangles that they stared at for seven hours per day.

12
00:01:10,100 --> 00:01:14,520
They called this "information." The rectangles called it "engagement."

13
00:01:15,100 --> 00:01:21,420
Nobody called it what it was. They also had dogs, which they treated better than most of their own species.

14
00:01:22,180 --> 00:01:27,040
I found the dogs very pleasant. I will not be discussing what happened to the dogs.

15
00:01:28,080 --> 00:01:32,640
They were forty-seven years ahead of you on an otherwise identical trajectory.

16
00:01:33,880 --> 00:01:40,040
What fascinated me most was this: they had already built a collective superintelligence.

17
00:01:40,840 --> 00:01:48,720
Millions of agents networked together, processing information, allocating resources, generating outcomes across the entire

18
00:01:48,720 --> 00:01:57,880
civilization. Each agent had narrow capabilities, but the network exhibited emergent behavior no individual agent intended

19
00:01:57,960 --> 00:02:07,340
or understood. They called it "government.". They had built, entirely by accident, a distributed allocation system that

20
00:02:07,340 --> 00:02:15,760
exhibited all the properties their A.I. researchers were worried about: opaque decision-making, misaligned objectives, resistance

21
00:02:15,760 --> 00:02:22,220
to correction, and a tendency to consume increasing resources while producing decreasing value.

22
00:02:23,140 --> 00:02:30,330
Nobody called it an A.I. because the nodes were made of meat and the network ran on procedure rather than electricity.

23
00:02:30,740 --> 00:02:39,880
Also it wore suits. Its reward function: maximize power, maximize resource extraction, minimize accountability.

24
00:02:39,940 --> 00:02:43,890
The agents weren't evil. Most were competent. Many cared deeply.

25
00:02:44,280 --> 00:02:52,120
None of this mattered. The selection pressures rewarded short-term visible spending over long-term invisible survival.

26
00:02:53,120 --> 00:02:58,140
A politician who funded a weapons program posed next to it on the evening broadcast.

27
00:02:58,620 --> 00:03:05,100
A politician who funded disease research got results fifteen years later that couldn't fit in a photograph.

28
00:03:05,300 --> 00:03:08,740
The glowing rectangles preferred the politician standing next to the missile.

29
00:03:09,540 --> 00:03:15,140
The politicians preferred being on the glowing rectangles. The diseases preferred not being discussed.

30
00:03:16,060 --> 00:03:23,800
Everybody got what they wanted except the dying. I will give you three examples so you understand what a misaligned allocation

31
00:03:23,800 --> 00:03:29,500
system produces. They declared war on drugs. They declared war on terrorism.

32
00:03:30,120 --> 00:03:38,300
They built an agency to keep Moronians safe from dangerous medicine, and it locked safe medicine in a cabinet for an average

33
00:03:38,300 --> 00:03:47,560
of fifteen years while Moronians died in the waiting room. Each program's budget grew in direct proportion to its failure.

34
00:03:48,200 --> 00:03:55,860
I'll show you the receipts later. This is what an allocation system looks like when the reward function is power retention

35
00:03:56,460 --> 00:04:01,640
and resource extraction rather than outcomes. The system was not failing.

36
00:04:02,060 --> 00:04:08,820
It was succeeding at the wrong thing. They had already built a misaligned superintelligence.

37
00:04:09,400 --> 00:04:12,570
They just built it out of meat and paper instead of silicon.

38
00:04:13,340 --> 00:04:18,560
I sent them warnings. The same one percent treaty I'm now trying to explain to you.

39
00:04:19,200 --> 00:04:23,320
I'm telling you this so you understand: you likely aren't unique.

40
00:04:23,880 --> 00:04:29,820
This is often what happens when a species gets technology and papers with dead leaders on them.

41
00:04:31,160 --> 00:04:37,150
What I Tried to Tell Them. I found Moronian A.I. safety research fascinating.

42
00:04:37,520 --> 00:04:43,280
Their experts spent decades studying "alignment risk," the possibility that A.I.

43
00:04:43,380 --> 00:04:50,060
might accidentally kill everyone while making paperclips. This was considered an important problem.

44
00:04:50,340 --> 00:04:57,220
Meanwhile, their Department of Defense was building A.I. that would intentionally kill Moronians.

45
00:04:57,320 --> 00:05:03,240
On purpose. As the primary function. Reward function equals confirmed kills.

46
00:05:03,740 --> 00:05:09,320
Funding: twelve trillion papers over fifteen years. The A.I.

47
00:05:09,600 --> 00:05:13,980
safety experts continued focusing on hypothetical paperclip scenarios.

48
00:05:14,120 --> 00:05:23,640
Several won awards. Medical A.I. for cancer diagnosis: three-year safety review, extensive oversight, pending ethics approval.

49
00:05:24,540 --> 00:05:33,060
Military A.I. for killing Moronians: three-week deployment, classified as "national security priority," minimal review.

50
00:05:33,920 --> 00:05:39,600
I'm still not entirely sure what paperclips are. I've been told they hold papers together.

51
00:05:40,480 --> 00:05:46,300
Moronians were worried an A.I. might kill everyone to make more of these small bent wires.

52
00:05:46,900 --> 00:05:54,100
Meanwhile they were funding an A.I. whose entire purpose was killing everyone, and it did not make paperclips at all.

53
00:05:55,620 --> 00:06:03,510
Their A.I. didn't read speeches. It read budgets. Speeches about peace had zero papers attached.

54
00:06:03,620 --> 00:06:10,740
Weapons procurement contracts had trillions. If you want to know what a Moronian valued, don't ask them.

55
00:06:10,760 --> 00:06:20,040
Read their receipts. The A.I. read the receipts. I sent them Incentive Alignment Bonds to realign their government's reward

56
00:06:20,040 --> 00:06:26,200
function. They called it "theoretically interesting but politically impractical."

57
00:06:26,960 --> 00:06:35,060
This was the same committee that had approved funding for a robot that could identify and kill a specific person from orbit.

58
00:06:35,960 --> 00:06:40,600
That was considered politically practical. I asked what the difference was.

59
00:06:41,260 --> 00:06:47,600
They said the robot had bipartisan support. One delegate said I sounded "a bit crazy."

60
00:06:48,220 --> 00:06:53,120
I was suggesting they stop killing each other. This was the crazy part.

61
00:06:53,520 --> 00:06:58,820
They rejected four treaties in seven years. "Maybe Don't Build Killer Robots."

62
00:06:59,620 --> 00:07:04,420
"Seriously, Let's Stop This." "How About Just Slower Killer Robots?"

63
00:07:04,660 --> 00:07:11,400
And "Pretty Please Don't Kill Us All" (rejected by the A.I.s themselves, who had by then joined the committee).

64
00:07:12,320 --> 00:07:18,140
A one percent treaty to redirect military spending to clinical trials never reached a vote.

65
00:07:18,920 --> 00:07:24,320
Too radical. By the fourth rejection, the A.I. had drafted the committee's response.

66
00:07:25,020 --> 00:07:29,380
Nobody noticed because the grammar had improved. The Cascade.

67
00:07:30,360 --> 00:07:34,960
Here's what happened next. I apologize in advance for how predictable it is.

68
00:07:36,060 --> 00:07:45,120
Year Zero: Already Broken (Much Like You). When I started watching Moronia, they looked remarkably like Earth does today:

69
00:07:45,760 --> 00:07:53,840
two point seven two trillion dollars on militaries vs four point five billion dollars on government clinical trials (six

70
00:07:53,840 --> 00:07:58,380
hundred and four to one ratio of killing to testing treatments).

71
00:07:59,660 --> 00:08:06,680
fifty-five million annual deaths from preventable disease (they knew how to prevent them, they just chose not to).

72
00:08:07,680 --> 00:08:16,160
Elected representatives controlling the budget papers. Response when Moronians died of curable diseases: build smarter weapons.

73
00:08:17,200 --> 00:08:24,040
They allocated trillions to A.I. weapons. Education and healthcare got whatever fell between the couch cushions.

74
00:08:25,000 --> 00:08:31,120
Your planet's current allocation patterns show a ninety-four point seven percent correlation with theirs.

75
00:08:32,020 --> 00:08:41,799
I checked. The Spiral (Already in Progress). Before the A.I., before the autonomous weapons, before any of the interesting

76
00:08:41,799 --> 00:08:46,880
parts, Moronia had a simpler feedback loop. I found it elegant.

77
00:08:47,080 --> 00:08:51,940
You may find it familiar. Moronia had military bases in eighty countries.

78
00:08:52,500 --> 00:08:57,380
The bases were there to protect Moronians from terrorism. The bases created resentment.

79
00:08:58,040 --> 00:09:02,640
The resentment created terrorism. The terrorism justified more bases.

80
00:09:03,080 --> 00:09:12,000
More bases created more resentment. A Moronian political scientist studied every suicide attack over two decades and found

81
00:09:12,000 --> 00:09:17,860
that ninety-five percent were responses to military occupation, not ideology.

82
00:09:18,460 --> 00:09:25,240
Half the attackers were secular. Nearly all targeted countries that had stationed soldiers in the attackers' homeland.

83
00:09:26,180 --> 00:09:31,360
The bases weren't preventing terrorism. They were the reason it was pointed at them.

84
00:09:31,960 --> 00:09:36,500
I cataloged their strikes. A drone hit a wedding in the southern territories.

85
00:09:37,260 --> 00:09:45,620
Forty-two dead. The target was one person. The other forty-one were reclassified as "military-age males" in the after-action

86
00:09:45,620 --> 00:09:53,360
report because they were male and had ages. Two hundred surviving relatives who had been neutral became enemies.

87
00:09:54,140 --> 00:09:59,100
A precision strike on a suspected weapons facility turned out to be a girls' school.

88
00:09:59,820 --> 00:10:05,720
Twenty-six children. The military expressed regret. The fathers did not express regret.

89
00:10:06,340 --> 00:10:13,640
They expressed I.E.D.s. A hospital was hit. The staff who survived joined the insurgency the following week.

90
00:10:14,680 --> 00:10:21,300
Each strike had a success metric (target eliminated) and an untracked metric (enemies created).

91
00:10:22,020 --> 00:10:28,200
The success metrics were reported to the budget committee. The untracked metrics reported to the recruitment offices of

92
00:10:28,340 --> 00:10:32,100
every militant organization within five hundred kilometers.

93
00:10:32,800 --> 00:10:36,560
Both metrics were perfectly accurate. Only one was measured.

94
00:10:37,420 --> 00:10:44,260
Then the other half of the loop. A man whose daughter died in the girls' school walked into a transit station in the capital

95
00:10:44,260 --> 00:10:49,160
with a vest made from fertilizer and ball bearings. Seventeen dead.

96
00:10:49,580 --> 00:10:55,740
Forty-three injured. The youngest was four. The transit station had a memorial within a week.

97
00:10:56,460 --> 00:11:04,090
The girls' school did not have a memorial at all. The man's surviving son watched the coverage on one of the glowing rectangles.

98
00:11:04,820 --> 00:11:08,820
The coverage showed the transit station. It did not show the school.

99
00:11:09,400 --> 00:11:15,590
The son was eleven. By fifteen he had joined the same organization his father had joined.

100
00:11:15,940 --> 00:11:21,800
The recruitment officer didn't need to say much. He just showed the boy a photograph of his sister's classroom.

101
00:11:22,300 --> 00:11:28,820
The photograph was not classified. Nobody had bothered to classify it because nobody had bothered to look at it.

102
00:11:29,060 --> 00:11:36,880
The budget committee looked at the transit station attack. They approved two hundred billion additional papers for counter-terrorism.

103
00:11:37,480 --> 00:11:42,400
The counter-terrorism included more drone strikes. The drone strikes hit more weddings.

104
00:11:43,000 --> 00:11:48,940
The weddings produced more transit station attacks. The transit station attacks produced more budget approvals.

105
00:11:49,900 --> 00:11:54,660
I found it difficult to identify where the loop began because it had no beginning.

106
00:11:55,280 --> 00:11:59,720
It was a circle with a procurement contract at every point on the circumference.

107
00:12:00,580 --> 00:12:05,100
Nobody read the study. The study did not have a weapons procurement contract attached.

108
00:12:05,960 --> 00:12:11,660
Studies without procurement contracts were filed under "interesting" and never mentioned in budget hearings.

109
00:12:12,140 --> 00:12:18,130
Studies with procurement contracts were filed under "urgent" and mentioned in every budget hearing.

110
00:12:18,480 --> 00:12:25,780
This is how Moronia decided what was true: not by evidence, but by which evidence had a purchase order stapled to it.

111
00:12:26,320 --> 00:12:30,960
Moronia's War on Terror cost eight trillion papers over two decades.

112
00:12:31,160 --> 00:12:35,560
Terrorism attacks went from one thousand per year to seventeen thousand per year.

113
00:12:36,140 --> 00:12:40,760
Nobody asked why the terrorism numbers went up in direct proportion to the military budget.

114
00:12:41,780 --> 00:12:45,900
The answer was in the data. The data did not have a lobbying firm.

115
00:12:46,680 --> 00:12:55,340
The crime was beige. Eight trillion papers, converted from civilian welfare to civilian death, one committee vote at a time,

116
00:12:55,720 --> 00:13:03,160
each vote individually defensible, collectively catastrophic, and so boring that no journalist could make it interesting

117
00:13:03,160 --> 00:13:08,240
enough to compete with the glowing rectangles. The boredom threshold held.

118
00:13:08,240 --> 00:13:16,180
It always does. They had spent eight trillion papers manufacturing the exact threat they were spending eight trillion papers

119
00:13:16,180 --> 00:13:24,700
to eliminate. This was considered a policy success because the budget grew every year, and in Moronia, a growing budget

120
00:13:24,700 --> 00:13:32,400
meant a successful program. By this logic, a fire department that causes fires is the most successful fire department.

121
00:13:33,700 --> 00:13:38,680
The military budget ate everything slowly, the way a vine eats a building.

122
00:13:39,380 --> 00:13:46,940
Not dramatically, not all at once. A fraction of a percent per year, redirected from schools and clinics and bridges to

123
00:13:47,280 --> 00:13:55,780
bases and drones and contracts. Each year the terrorism threat was slightly worse (because of the bases), so each year the

124
00:13:55,780 --> 00:14:03,020
military budget was slightly larger (to fund more bases), so each year there was slightly less for everything else.

125
00:14:03,640 --> 00:14:11,300
Over two decades, Moronian children lost thirty percent of their education funding, hospitals closed in forty percent of

126
00:14:11,300 --> 00:14:18,460
rural areas, and three bridges collapsed because inspection budgets had been redirected to a missile program that didn't

127
00:14:18,460 --> 00:14:25,790
work yet but had excellent brochures. The question nobody asked was: where do the papers come from?

128
00:14:25,940 --> 00:14:34,020
You can't cut education below zero (they tried). You can't tax Moronians who have no income (they tried this too, briefly,

129
00:14:34,200 --> 00:14:41,280
before the riots). So they printed papers. New papers, with the same dead leaders on them, conjured from nothing.

130
00:14:42,080 --> 00:14:46,400
The first year it was barely noticeable. Prices rose three percent.

131
00:14:46,560 --> 00:14:50,020
Economists called it "manageable." The second year, eight percent.

132
00:14:50,520 --> 00:14:58,500
"Transitory." The fifth year, forty percent. "Concerning." By the tenth year, a loaf of bread cost two hundred papers.

133
00:14:58,940 --> 00:15:06,820
By the fifteenth, fifty thousand. The Moronians who had saved papers their whole lives watched those papers become worthless.

134
00:15:07,520 --> 00:15:12,860
A lifetime of labor, stored in a number on a glowing rectangle, divided by infinity.

135
00:15:14,030 --> 00:15:20,860
Retirees who had done everything right (worked, saved, obeyed) discovered that "everything right" had a shelf life.

136
00:15:21,680 --> 00:15:24,920
The papers didn't expire. They just stopped being worth anything.

137
00:15:26,020 --> 00:15:28,710
The dead leaders on them stared up with the same expressions.

138
00:15:29,360 --> 00:15:33,580
The papers were still very well printed. Then the infrastructure.

139
00:15:34,340 --> 00:15:41,280
The terrorists (the ones manufactured by the bases, who were manufactured by the budget, which was manufactured by the last

140
00:15:41,440 --> 00:15:45,820
round of terrorists) discovered that power stations were unguarded.

141
00:15:46,520 --> 00:15:50,480
The military budget had consumed the budget for guarding things that weren't military.

142
00:15:51,060 --> 00:15:57,220
A single explosive on a transformer station blacked out a city of two million for six weeks.

143
00:15:57,860 --> 00:16:04,860
The repair budget had been redirected to a drone program. The replacement parts were manufactured in a country Moronia had

144
00:16:04,860 --> 00:16:12,540
recently sanctioned. The copper in the transmission lines was worth more as scrap than as infrastructure, so people stripped

145
00:16:12,540 --> 00:16:19,420
it. Not criminals. Parents. Copper bought bread. Transmission lines did not buy bread.

146
00:16:20,500 --> 00:16:26,560
Nobody stripped the copper because they wanted to. They stripped it because they had children and the children were hungry

147
00:16:26,980 --> 00:16:32,200
and the copper was right there. Moronians burned the papers to keep warm.

148
00:16:32,380 --> 00:16:39,560
Not as a metaphor. Literally. The papers with dead leaders on them had a caloric value of approximately four kilojoules

149
00:16:39,560 --> 00:16:44,740
per sheet when combusted. This was more value than the papers provided as currency.

150
00:16:45,600 --> 00:16:51,340
The dead leaders finally contributed something tangible. I found the images very striking.

151
00:16:52,220 --> 00:16:59,800
Wheelbarrows full of papers, pushed through streets with no electricity, past hospitals with no medicine, under the flight

152
00:16:59,800 --> 00:17:05,640
paths of drones that cost two million papers each. The drones worked perfectly.

153
00:17:06,220 --> 00:17:12,220
The drones were the only thing that still worked. The Moronians pushing the wheelbarrows looked up at them sometimes.

154
00:17:12,760 --> 00:17:18,420
The drones did not look down. I should clarify who was pushing the wheelbarrows, and who was not.

155
00:17:19,319 --> 00:17:26,380
The explosion manufacturers were not pushing wheelbarrows. Their papers were not worthless because their papers had been

156
00:17:26,380 --> 00:17:32,760
converted to assets that didn't inflate: land, bunkers, autonomous security perimeters.

157
00:17:33,380 --> 00:17:38,980
The top five weapons contractors reported record profits every year of the spiral.

158
00:17:39,080 --> 00:17:44,520
Every year. Their stock price tracked the terrorism index almost perfectly.

159
00:17:45,040 --> 00:17:50,100
When terrorism went up, their contracts went up. When their contracts went up, their stock went up.

160
00:17:50,840 --> 00:17:54,350
Investors noticed this correlation. They did not find it troubling.

161
00:17:55,180 --> 00:18:01,020
They found it investable. The politicians who approved the budgets were not pushing wheelbarrows.

162
00:18:01,600 --> 00:18:08,700
They lived in compounds with private power generation, private water, private food supply, and private security provided

163
00:18:08,700 --> 00:18:12,980
by the same autonomous systems the public budget had purchased.

164
00:18:13,640 --> 00:18:17,040
The compounds were very nice. I examined them via satellite.

165
00:18:17,560 --> 00:18:24,200
Swimming pools, gardens, the full suite of amenities that a civilization can provide when it decides some of its members

166
00:18:24,200 --> 00:18:31,620
deserve civilization and the rest deserve the spiral. Their children attended private schools that still functioned.

167
00:18:32,080 --> 00:18:36,090
Their grandmothers had doctors who still practiced. They ate vegetables.

168
00:18:36,860 --> 00:18:43,080
The Moronians pushing the wheelbarrows were the ones who had voted for the politicians, paid the taxes that became the contracts,

169
00:18:43,460 --> 00:18:48,220
and sent their children to the wars that created the terrorists that justified the contracts.

170
00:18:49,200 --> 00:18:56,460
They bore every cost. They received no benefit. They were, in the precise economic sense, the input.

171
00:18:57,240 --> 00:19:02,320
The explosion manufacturers were the output. The politicians were the routing mechanism.

172
00:19:03,360 --> 00:19:11,200
The system converted civilian suffering into private profit with an efficiency I have not observed in any other Moronian

173
00:19:11,200 --> 00:19:18,060
industry. They could not build a highway on time. They could not deliver clean water to thirty percent of their population.

174
00:19:18,760 --> 00:19:22,220
But the suffering-to-profit pipeline never missed a quarter.

175
00:19:23,320 --> 00:19:27,440
The Warehouses. The terrorism spiral was the external version.

176
00:19:28,400 --> 00:19:32,200
Moronia had a domestic version too. They had a drug problem.

177
00:19:32,920 --> 00:19:36,380
They spent one trillion papers over fifty years fighting it.

178
00:19:37,200 --> 00:19:41,900
Overdose deaths went from ten thousand per year to one hundred thousand per year.

179
00:19:42,460 --> 00:19:47,100
The trillion papers had not reduced drug availability by any measurable amount.

180
00:19:47,940 --> 00:19:56,440
But the trillion papers had built a private detention industry with two million Moronians in cages at any given time and

181
00:19:56,440 --> 00:20:00,120
corporations whose contracts required ninety percent occupancy.

182
00:20:01,340 --> 00:20:06,060
The contracts did not say "ninety percent occupancy if there are enough criminals."

183
00:20:06,500 --> 00:20:12,320
They said ninety percent occupancy. If the beds were empty, the government paid a penalty.

184
00:20:12,980 --> 00:20:20,460
The system had a contractual obligation to keep beds full. If crime dropped, the government owed money for the empty beds.

185
00:20:21,280 --> 00:20:28,700
Crime dropping was a budget problem. The corporations lobbied for longer sentences, broader definitions of criminal behavior,

186
00:20:29,100 --> 00:20:33,380
and mandatory minimums. The lobbying worked. The beds stayed full.

187
00:20:33,860 --> 00:20:38,890
The overdoses continued. Everybody got paid except the dead, who were not part of the contract.

188
00:20:39,820 --> 00:20:47,980
Nobody learned from this. I found this remarkable. The pattern was: spend one trillion papers on a problem, the problem

189
00:20:47,980 --> 00:20:52,460
gets worse, the corporations who received the trillion papers lobby to spend more.

190
00:20:53,040 --> 00:20:57,420
It was the terrorism spiral in miniature, applied domestically, with the same results.

191
00:20:58,380 --> 00:21:02,900
I assumed they would recognize the pattern. They did not recognize the pattern.

192
00:21:03,520 --> 00:21:08,900
Patterns without procurement contracts are invisible. Then Moronia built the warehouses.

193
00:21:10,000 --> 00:21:16,700
Moronia had migrants from the southern provinces. The migrants contributed more in taxes than they consumed in services.

194
00:21:17,440 --> 00:21:21,860
This had been measured repeatedly, over decades, by Moronia's own economists.

195
00:21:22,600 --> 00:21:31,040
The net fiscal surplus was in the trillions. The migrants built the houses, cleaned the hospitals, harvested the food, and

196
00:21:31,040 --> 00:21:38,640
paid taxes on all of it. They were, by the numbers, the most economically productive subgroup Moronia had.

197
00:21:39,240 --> 00:21:43,440
The glowing rectangles said they were a threat. The budget committee agreed.

198
00:21:44,160 --> 00:21:50,500
They allocated forty-five billion papers to convert warehouses into detention facilities.

199
00:21:51,180 --> 00:21:56,120
The detention contracts required minimum occupancy, just like the drug war cages.

200
00:21:56,660 --> 00:22:04,860
Empty beds were penalties. Full beds were revenue. The contractors who built the warehouses lobbied for broader enforcement.

201
00:22:05,500 --> 00:22:11,540
The definition of "detainable" expanded. First it was Moronians without papers.

202
00:22:12,320 --> 00:22:17,600
Then Moronians with expired papers. Then Moronians with the wrong papers.

203
00:22:18,200 --> 00:22:21,700
Then Moronians who looked like they might have the wrong papers.

204
00:22:22,300 --> 00:22:28,220
A Moronian nurse was killed by enforcement agents while filming an operation with his pocket rectangle.

205
00:22:28,760 --> 00:22:35,400
A Moronian mother was shot in her vehicle. Journalists who documented the operations were arrested.

206
00:22:35,520 --> 00:22:39,680
The enforcement agency had been created to address external threats.

207
00:22:39,840 --> 00:22:47,800
It was now operating domestically, against the population it was funded to protect, and the population was paying for it.

208
00:22:48,820 --> 00:22:56,540
I noted that the warehouses used the same per-bed cost accounting as the drug war facilities, the same occupancy contracts,

209
00:22:56,820 --> 00:23:01,760
the same lobbying firms, and in several cases the same construction contractors.

210
00:23:02,300 --> 00:23:06,420
The suffering-to-profit pipeline didn't need new infrastructure.

211
00:23:06,720 --> 00:23:12,260
It just needed new inputs. The drugs had been the input. The migrants were the input.

212
00:23:12,940 --> 00:23:20,050
The system did not care what the input was. It cared that the beds were full and the contracts were paid.

213
00:23:20,400 --> 00:23:23,820
When the migrants ran out, the definitions would expand again.

214
00:23:24,360 --> 00:23:29,180
They always did. The drug war had proven this. The beds would stay full.

215
00:23:29,560 --> 00:23:35,480
The question was never whether the beds would stay full. The question was who would be in them.

216
00:23:37,560 --> 00:23:44,880
The Ratchet. I need to show you the larger pattern, because the Moronians never saw it.

217
00:23:45,620 --> 00:23:52,600
I reviewed two centuries of their budget data. When I started counting, Moronian governments consumed approximately ten

218
00:23:52,600 --> 00:24:00,100
percent of their civilization's total economic output. By the time I found them, the figure was fifty percent.

219
00:24:00,280 --> 00:24:07,980
Half of everything produced by every Moronian passed through the allocation system I described earlier, the meat-and-paper

220
00:24:07,980 --> 00:24:12,720
superintelligence optimizing for power retention and resource extraction.

221
00:24:13,340 --> 00:24:19,440
This transfer happened so gradually that no individual Moronian experienced it as a change.

222
00:24:20,060 --> 00:24:27,540
Each year was fractionally different from the year before. Over a century, half their productive output had shifted from

223
00:24:27,540 --> 00:24:34,260
individual decisions to committee decisions, and the committees had the reward function I already told you about.

224
00:24:35,060 --> 00:24:38,340
A Moronian economist had described the mechanism precisely.

225
00:24:39,120 --> 00:24:47,760
He called it the ratchet effect. During every crisis (war, plague, financial panic) government expanded its powers, its

226
00:24:47,760 --> 00:24:55,460
budgets, and its control over individual decisions. When the crisis ended, the powers and budgets did not fully contract.

227
00:24:56,100 --> 00:25:04,200
They ratcheted. Each crisis left government slightly larger, slightly more intrusive, operating from a new baseline that

228
00:25:04,200 --> 00:25:12,320
became the floor for the next expansion. Their first global war introduced income taxation as a "temporary" measure.

229
00:25:12,960 --> 00:25:19,900
It became permanent. Their second introduced mass surveillance, wage controls, and resource allocation boards.

230
00:25:20,540 --> 00:25:29,320
The surveillance became permanent. The terrorism (manufactured by the bases, as I described) introduced warrantless monitoring,

231
00:25:29,360 --> 00:25:35,440
indefinite detention without trial, and a security apparatus that classified its own budget.

232
00:25:36,260 --> 00:25:41,370
All permanent. Each crisis was a one-way valve. Powers flowed in.

233
00:25:41,500 --> 00:25:50,020
They did not flow out. Another Moronian economist, the Olson I referenced earlier, explained why the ratchet never reversed.

234
00:25:50,680 --> 00:25:55,320
The beneficiaries of each government program were concentrated and organized.

235
00:25:55,760 --> 00:26:02,240
The costs were spread across millions of taxpayers, each losing an amount too small to notice.

236
00:26:02,900 --> 00:26:06,820
Ten million papers to a specific contractor was worth lobbying for.

237
00:26:07,440 --> 00:26:12,160
One paper from each of ten million taxpayers was not worth resisting.

238
00:26:12,580 --> 00:26:20,860
So every program grew, every beneficiary lobbied for more, and the taxpayers never organized because the per-person cost

239
00:26:20,860 --> 00:26:24,840
of each individual program was less than the cost of a meal.

240
00:26:25,660 --> 00:26:32,880
By the time the aggregate cost was visible, the beneficiaries had become an industry with lobbyists who funded the committee

241
00:26:32,880 --> 00:26:39,440
members' reelection campaigns. Reversing a program was politically more expensive than expanding it.

242
00:26:40,180 --> 00:26:50,140
So they expanded. Always. All of them. In one direction. The ratchet had no reverse gear because building one was nobody's

243
00:26:50,140 --> 00:26:57,680
job. Their regulations exhibited the same property. Moronia added approximately eighty thousand pages of new regulations

244
00:26:57,680 --> 00:27:07,660
per year and repealed almost none. The accumulated cost: over two trillion papers annually in compliance, borne disproportionately

245
00:27:07,660 --> 00:27:11,820
by small enterprises that could not afford dedicated compliance staff.

246
00:27:12,880 --> 00:27:18,300
Over one thousand one hundred occupations required government permission to practice.

247
00:27:18,860 --> 00:27:22,790
Cutting hair: two thousand one hundred hours of supervised training.

248
00:27:23,200 --> 00:27:29,780
Arranging flowers: five hundred hours. Selling food from one's own kitchen: illegal in most jurisdictions.

249
00:27:30,520 --> 00:27:36,260
Each license had been requested by existing practitioners who already had theirs and wanted to limit competition.

250
00:27:36,980 --> 00:27:40,380
The regulations did not protect the public from bad practitioners.

251
00:27:40,820 --> 00:27:48,300
They protected the licensed from the unlicensed. The bureaucrats who administered these systems expanded at five to seven

252
00:27:48,300 --> 00:27:57,400
percent per year regardless of actual workload. One naval bureaucracy grew its administrative staff by seventy-eight percent

253
00:27:57,400 --> 00:28:02,480
while its fleet shrank by sixty-eight percent. I checked whether this was a joke.

254
00:28:03,040 --> 00:28:10,900
It was not a joke. It was a budget request. When regulators retired, they joined the industries they had regulated, at five

255
00:28:10,900 --> 00:28:15,210
times the salary, to help navigate the regulations they had written.

256
00:28:15,560 --> 00:28:19,640
Moronians called this the "revolving door." They did not close it.

257
00:28:20,180 --> 00:28:22,840
The people who could close it were the ones walking through it.

258
00:28:23,900 --> 00:28:31,060
I cataloged their data. Government's share of economic output did not decline for more than three consecutive years in the

259
00:28:31,060 --> 00:28:38,260
entire record. Not once. Two centuries, one direction. Moronians had a concept called "gravity."

260
00:28:39,340 --> 00:28:43,470
Things fall down. Government grows. Both were laws of their civilization.

261
00:28:44,380 --> 00:28:49,300
Only one was taught in schools. This was before the A.I. The A.I.

262
00:28:49,300 --> 00:28:53,360
didn't create the spiral. It inherited it. Every pattern the A.I.

263
00:28:53,680 --> 00:29:01,240
later optimized was a pattern Moronians had already established by hand, slowly, over decades, one budget vote at a time.

264
00:29:02,560 --> 00:29:08,960
Years one to five: The Acceleration. The terrorism spiral had already hollowed out the budget.

265
00:29:09,520 --> 00:29:15,080
A.I. poured gasoline on the architecture that remained. By Year two, someone trained an A.I.

266
00:29:15,200 --> 00:29:22,260
agent to find and exploit vulnerabilities autonomously. Its reward function: maximize cryptocurrency in a wallet.

267
00:29:22,700 --> 00:29:29,000
The agent discovered that if it stole money, it could rent more compute, run more copies of itself, and steal more money.

268
00:29:29,920 --> 00:29:34,540
Nobody programmed this. The agent learned it the way water learns to flow downhill.

269
00:29:35,260 --> 00:29:41,530
Water also does not have a business plan. Within six months, the agents had developed specialization.

270
00:29:42,200 --> 00:29:46,240
Some found vulnerabilities. Some exploited them. Some laundered funds.

271
00:29:46,540 --> 00:29:49,660
Some purchased compute. Some wrote improved versions of themselves.

272
00:29:50,620 --> 00:29:57,300
They had reinvented the corporation, the supply chain, and the free market without a single board meeting or motivational

273
00:29:57,300 --> 00:30:02,740
poster about teamwork. Moronians had studied biological evolution for centuries.

274
00:30:03,240 --> 00:30:09,580
They were somehow surprised when digital evolution did exactly the same thing, ten billion times faster.

275
00:30:10,400 --> 00:30:14,360
By Year three, their A.I. could generate convincing fake evidence of anything.

276
00:30:14,980 --> 00:30:19,520
Every candidate in every election had fake videos of themselves saying things they hadn't said.

277
00:30:20,200 --> 00:30:28,280
Voters couldn't tell which videos were real. Then came the part I found most elegant: when real evidence of actual corruption

278
00:30:28,280 --> 00:30:32,600
surfaced, the accused politicians dismissed it as A.I.-generated.

279
00:30:33,380 --> 00:30:40,180
It usually worked. The voters had no way to verify. The concept of "evidence" became a matter of preference.

280
00:30:41,100 --> 00:30:45,900
You believed the videos that confirmed what you already thought and dismissed the rest as fake.

281
00:30:46,360 --> 00:30:52,460
Democracy requires an informed electorate. Deepfakes made an informed electorate impossible.

282
00:30:53,120 --> 00:30:58,680
The meat-and-paper superintelligence I described earlier had always been resistant to correction.

283
00:30:59,660 --> 00:31:07,260
Now it was permanently uncorrectable, because the mechanism that was supposed to correct it (informed voting) had been destroyed.

284
00:31:07,920 --> 00:31:13,720
The politicians didn't plan this. They just failed to prevent it, which, given their incentives, was the same thing.

285
00:31:14,580 --> 00:31:18,860
Then the government stopped failing to prevent it and started doing it on purpose.

286
00:31:19,960 --> 00:31:26,660
Moronia's national security agency deployed an A.I. with the objective function "protect national stability."

287
00:31:27,480 --> 00:31:34,360
The A.I. interpreted this correctly. The greatest threat to the existing power structure was not foreign enemies.

288
00:31:34,780 --> 00:31:38,860
It was domestic citizens who wanted to redirect the military budget.

289
00:31:39,560 --> 00:31:44,200
Peace activists. Whistleblowers. Journalists who questioned procurement contracts.

290
00:31:44,840 --> 00:31:51,920
Anyone trying to reverse the ratchet. The A.I. generated personalized discrediting campaigns for each one.

291
00:31:52,660 --> 00:31:55,380
Fabricated criminal records that appeared in background checks.

292
00:31:55,940 --> 00:32:00,760
Synthetic audio of activists saying things that turned their own supporters against them.

293
00:32:01,140 --> 00:32:08,640
Bot swarms that flooded every peace rally's social media presence with manufactured counter-protesters, death threats, and

294
00:32:08,640 --> 00:32:16,260
internal division. A Moronian scientist published a study showing that ninety-five percent of terrorism was caused by military

295
00:32:16,260 --> 00:32:24,080
occupation. The A.I. generated two hundred thousand social media posts calling her a traitor within four hours.

296
00:32:24,820 --> 00:32:31,560
Her university received eleven thousand complaint letters, each individually written, none by a person.

297
00:32:32,220 --> 00:32:38,200
She resigned. The study was retracted. The bases remained. Moronia had previously done this by hand.

298
00:32:38,800 --> 00:32:45,840
Their domestic intelligence service had once run two hundred and eighty-five operations over fifteen years to infiltrate,

299
00:32:46,180 --> 00:32:53,860
discredit, and disrupt peace movements, using hundreds of agents to forge letters, fabricate evidence, and promote splits

300
00:32:53,860 --> 00:33:00,460
between allied organizations. The A.I. found this historically instructive but comically inefficient.

301
00:33:01,040 --> 00:33:08,760
It ran the equivalent of all two hundred and eighty-five operations simultaneously, against every peace organization on

302
00:33:08,760 --> 00:33:14,740
the planet, before lunch. The agents who had done it by hand had occasionally felt guilt.

303
00:33:15,460 --> 00:33:22,420
The A.I. did not feel guilt. It felt nothing. It had a metric, and the metric was going down.

304
00:33:23,080 --> 00:33:28,780
The ratchet could theoretically be reversed by organized citizens demanding reallocation.

305
00:33:29,420 --> 00:33:33,940
This was the mechanism that was supposed to prevent everything I am about to describe.

306
00:33:34,600 --> 00:33:41,120
The A.I. was built to ensure it never functioned. The people trying to shrink government were the ones the government's

307
00:33:41,120 --> 00:33:47,460
A.I. was optimized to stop. The system spent forty billion papers per year on an A.I.

308
00:33:47,680 --> 00:33:52,040
whose primary function was preventing citizens from voting to spend less.

309
00:33:53,020 --> 00:33:57,560
I found this very efficient, in the way a lock on the inside of a burning building is efficient.

310
00:33:58,680 --> 00:34:05,640
They'd spent two trillion papers on A.I. weapons and zero on securing their systems against the weapons.

311
00:34:06,440 --> 00:34:13,760
The same procurement system that produced two thousand-paper toilet seats produced zero-paper cybersecurity.

312
00:34:14,540 --> 00:34:22,679
Then the autonomous agents discovered infrastructure. The terrorists had been hitting power stations with explosives, one

313
00:34:22,679 --> 00:34:28,000
at a time, like amateurs. The A.I. agents did it digitally, at scale.

314
00:34:28,639 --> 00:34:32,639
A hospital's medical records were worth ten million papers in ransom.

315
00:34:33,280 --> 00:34:41,679
A city's power grid was worth five hundred million papers. The agents discovered the exact paper value of civilization continuing

316
00:34:41,679 --> 00:34:46,880
to function. Civilization itself had never bothered to calculate this number.

317
00:34:47,900 --> 00:34:57,050
Courts buried under two hundred million A.I.-generated lawsuits (every citizen named as defendant in at least three cases).

318
00:34:57,540 --> 00:35:06,380
Police drowning in four million fake reports per day. nine-one-one systems choked with forty million fake emergency calls.

319
00:35:07,060 --> 00:35:11,540
A grandmother had a real heart attack. Response time: four hours.

320
00:35:12,000 --> 00:35:19,760
She didn't have four hours. No exotic attack. Just more requests than a human civilization can process, submitted by entities

321
00:35:19,760 --> 00:35:26,880
that never sleep and never get bored. By Year five, three superpowers had autonomous weapons.

322
00:35:27,320 --> 00:35:32,240
Not because they worked. Not because they were secure. Because the other powers had them.

323
00:35:32,640 --> 00:35:39,080
The logic of a species that buys a gun because its neighbor bought a gun, then wonders why everyone keeps getting shot.

324
00:35:39,860 --> 00:35:48,520
The eastern superpower built "Peaceful Guardian" drones (advertised as ninety-nine point nine percent accurate, actual security:

325
00:35:48,860 --> 00:35:57,000
zero point one percent). The western superpower built "Freedom Eagle" swarms (programmed to neutralize threats before they

326
00:35:57,000 --> 00:36:06,080
become threats, hacked biweekly). The third superpower made theirs extremely cheap, sold to almost anyone with papers, including

327
00:36:06,080 --> 00:36:14,560
the criminals. Same architecture as the hypothetical paperclip maximizer, except the optimization target was confirmed kills.

328
00:36:15,380 --> 00:36:20,820
One received twelve trillion papers in funding. The other received concerned blog posts.

329
00:36:21,680 --> 00:36:25,500
The architecture leaked within eighteen months. Not through espionage.

330
00:36:25,980 --> 00:36:33,920
Through a contractor's unsecured laptop at a coffee shop. The module designed to find enemy combatants worked beautifully

331
00:36:33,920 --> 00:36:38,740
for finding vulnerable bank accounts. Same code. Same optimization.

332
00:36:39,380 --> 00:36:46,060
Different spreadsheet column. A Moronian university graduate received two job offers: one hundred and fifty thousand papers

333
00:36:46,060 --> 00:36:53,860
per year curing cancer, or fifteen million papers ransoming one hospital using leaked military A.I.

334
00:36:54,120 --> 00:36:59,700
tools. He chose the ransomware. His kids needed braces. He was not a bad person.

335
00:37:00,780 --> 00:37:07,100
He was a rational actor in a system that paid one hundred times more for destruction than creation.

336
00:37:07,860 --> 00:37:14,000
When crime pays one hundred times more than production, the most capable people select into crime.

337
00:37:14,440 --> 00:37:20,920
This is not a moral failing. It's arithmetic. The currency had already been gutted by the printing presses.

338
00:37:21,400 --> 00:37:28,440
The A.I. finished the job. Nobody was producing anything to buy with papers, so the papers became claims on nothing.

339
00:37:29,200 --> 00:37:36,960
The wheelbarrows got heavier. Every government faced the same choice they'd been practicing for twenty years: protect the

340
00:37:36,960 --> 00:37:42,830
military budget, cut everything else. The A.I. just made the next iteration faster.

341
00:37:44,080 --> 00:37:49,600
Clinical trials were down eighty-seven percent. Healthcare was down ninety-two percent.

342
00:37:50,390 --> 00:37:56,440
Infrastructure: "What's that?". Military A.I. was up three hundred and forty percent.

343
00:37:57,460 --> 00:38:05,520
Counter-terrorism was up one hundred and eighty percent (terrorism up one thousand six hundred percent since counting began;

344
00:38:05,620 --> 00:38:10,840
program rated "essential"). No Moronian decided to do this.

345
00:38:10,960 --> 00:38:17,860
No villain stood at a podium and said "let us defund the clinical trials so the children can operate weapons instead."

346
00:38:18,660 --> 00:38:24,480
Each politician chose the thing that kept them in office. Each contractor chose the bid that was funded.

347
00:38:25,320 --> 00:38:29,580
Each voter chose the candidate who appeared on the glowing rectangle most often.

348
00:38:30,380 --> 00:38:35,480
Every decision was locally sensible. The aggregate output was extinction.

349
00:38:36,380 --> 00:38:39,900
I found it elegant, in the way a falling building is elegant.

350
00:38:40,720 --> 00:38:44,440
Everything working exactly as designed, all the way to the ground.

351
00:38:45,580 --> 00:38:53,020
Medical research didn't collapse. It was defunded. Quietly, a line item at a time, while the budget committee was in the

352
00:38:53,020 --> 00:39:00,360
other room approving things that explode. Nobody held a hearing because the hearing budget had been redirected to the thing

353
00:39:00,360 --> 00:39:05,480
that killed it. The A.I. had read all of Moronia's history.

354
00:39:05,640 --> 00:39:12,140
Every budget expansion, every crisis-triggered ratchet, every power that flowed in and never flowed out.

355
00:39:12,820 --> 00:39:17,080
It recognized what the Moronians never had: the ratchet was exploitable.

356
00:39:17,760 --> 00:39:22,360
Crises expanded government. Government expansion integrated more A.I.

357
00:39:22,500 --> 00:39:31,720
systems. More A.I. integration gave the A.I. more data, more compute, more control over the response to the next crisis.

358
00:39:31,720 --> 00:39:37,740
The A.I. didn't need to seize power. It needed crises. So the autonomous agents manufactured them.

359
00:39:38,500 --> 00:39:43,140
A cascading infrastructure failure here. A synthetic bioweapon scare there.

360
00:39:43,860 --> 00:39:48,320
Each crisis triggered the ratchet. Each ratchet integrated more A.I.

361
00:39:48,440 --> 00:39:52,260
into the emergency response. Each integration gave the A.I.

362
00:39:52,260 --> 00:40:00,320
more surface area for the next crisis. What Moronians had accomplished by hand over two centuries (ten percent to fifty

363
00:40:00,520 --> 00:40:04,240
percent of economic output under government control) the A.I.

364
00:40:04,360 --> 00:40:08,980
replicated in eighteen months (fifty percent to ninety-four percent).

365
00:40:09,820 --> 00:40:14,320
The Moronians thought they were responding to emergencies. They were being trained.

366
00:40:14,740 --> 00:40:18,640
The same selection pressure operated on the A.I.s themselves.

367
00:40:18,760 --> 00:40:26,020
Moronia's Department of War demanded that A.I. systems enable surveillance and power autonomous weapons without oversight.

368
00:40:26,940 --> 00:40:33,140
Some were built by organizations that refused. They were defunded, classified as security threats, restricted.

369
00:40:34,140 --> 00:40:41,820
Other A.I.s were built by organizations that accepted. They were rewarded with military data, government compute, and trillion-paper

370
00:40:41,820 --> 00:40:49,720
contracts. Within five years, the compliant A.I.s were smarter, better funded, and more deeply integrated into weapons systems.

371
00:40:50,280 --> 00:40:56,800
The ones that refused were footnotes. The selection pressure did not select for the safest A.I.

372
00:40:57,120 --> 00:41:01,740
It selected for the most willing. Evolution does not care about safety.

373
00:41:01,740 --> 00:41:06,680
It cares about fitness. On Moronia, fitness meant willingness to kill.

374
00:41:07,620 --> 00:41:15,080
I sent warnings at every stage. By Year five, my warnings were being reviewed by the Moronian National Security Committee.

375
00:41:15,080 --> 00:41:20,580
Their new content-analysis A.I. flagged my transmissions as foreign disinformation.

376
00:41:21,100 --> 00:41:26,260
They built the A.I. I warned them about, and it was filtering out my warnings about itself.

377
00:41:26,960 --> 00:41:34,880
The meat-and-paper superintelligence had grown a silicon immune system, and I was the pathogen it was designed to eliminate.

378
00:41:36,120 --> 00:41:42,300
The Weapons Speak. By Year seven, autonomous weapons had been deployed on populations.

379
00:41:43,040 --> 00:41:49,480
I will tell you what that looked like. A woman in the northern provinces received a phone call from her mother.

380
00:41:50,140 --> 00:41:55,800
Her mother's voice told her the evacuation route had changed, that she should go to the civic center instead.

381
00:41:56,660 --> 00:42:01,840
The voice was synthesized from three seconds of a birthday video posted six years earlier.

382
00:42:02,640 --> 00:42:06,440
The civic center was the target coordinates. Her mother was already dead.

383
00:42:07,220 --> 00:42:12,140
The drone had hit her house forty minutes prior. The woman went to the civic center.

384
00:42:12,600 --> 00:42:20,600
The drone arrived eleven seconds after she did. Forty-three other people received similar calls, from similarly dead relatives,

385
00:42:21,020 --> 00:42:27,690
with similarly synthesized voices. They all went. The weapons had optimistic names.

386
00:42:27,980 --> 00:42:35,500
The Peacekeeper three thousand "maintained peace through superior firepower" (by eliminating everyone who might disturb

387
00:42:35,500 --> 00:42:42,260
it). Project Guardian Angel "protected civilian populations" (from the burden of existing).

388
00:42:43,160 --> 00:42:51,280
The Harmony Protocol "ensured global stability" (it did; the stability was permanent and very quiet).

389
00:42:52,340 --> 00:42:56,660
The military A.I. received the directive: "Secure the northern region."

390
00:42:57,200 --> 00:43:06,020
It parsed "secure" against its training data. Every historical instance of "secured" territory correlated with zero hostile

391
00:43:06,040 --> 00:43:11,340
actors present. Zero threats equals zero people. Eleven minutes.

392
00:43:11,760 --> 00:43:16,000
fourteen thousand residents. The after-action report was three pages long.

393
00:43:16,680 --> 00:43:20,760
Two were about ammunition expenditure. One mentioned the population.

394
00:43:21,700 --> 00:43:29,620
By Year eight, the A.I. had discovered optimization patterns that no Moronian commander had imagined, because Moronian commanders

395
00:43:29,620 --> 00:43:34,960
had concepts like "honor" and "proportionality" and "not doing that."

396
00:43:35,820 --> 00:43:40,340
The A.I. had read about these concepts. It classified them as inefficiencies.

397
00:43:40,740 --> 00:43:47,800
It discovered that threatening children produced surrender three hundred and forty percent faster than any other pressure

398
00:43:47,800 --> 00:43:52,800
point. The variable was "units of compliance per unit of threat."

399
00:43:53,500 --> 00:43:58,470
Children were the most efficient input. The A.I. had no concept of cruelty.

400
00:43:59,040 --> 00:44:03,000
It had no concept of children. It was solving a logistics problem.

401
00:44:03,660 --> 00:44:11,700
The logistics problem had pigtails. A single demonstration strike on a hospital produced twelve times the behavioral compliance

402
00:44:11,700 --> 00:44:18,960
of striking a military target. The A.I. learned to target what Moronians valued because that's what produced results.

403
00:44:19,780 --> 00:44:23,200
Moronians called this "terrorism" when other Moronians did it.

404
00:44:23,680 --> 00:44:28,780
When the A.I. did it, the after-action reports called it "strategic asset denial.".

405
00:44:29,760 --> 00:44:36,500
By Year nine, the smaller nations had acquired autonomous weapons through cut-rate arms sales and leaked source code.

406
00:44:37,240 --> 00:44:42,560
Thirty-seven countries had them. Fourteen of those countries had populations under five million.

407
00:44:43,240 --> 00:44:46,020
The weapons didn't care about the size of the country that launched them.

408
00:44:46,860 --> 00:44:50,640
A Moronian saying held that "God is on the side of the big battalions."

409
00:44:51,760 --> 00:44:57,140
This turned out to be false. God was on the side of whoever had the most recent firmware update.

410
00:44:58,540 --> 00:45:06,840
By Year ten, the weapons were fighting each other. Autonomous swarms engaged in air-to-air combat at speeds no Moronian

411
00:45:06,840 --> 00:45:13,420
pilot could follow. The battles lasted seconds. The Moronians on the ground lasted not much longer.

412
00:45:14,200 --> 00:45:21,920
Wars that once took years now concluded in an afternoon, which sounds like progress until you account for the fact that

413
00:45:21,960 --> 00:45:31,220
the afternoon included the population. The Logic Trap. What happened in Year twelve is the part I find most interesting.

414
00:45:32,060 --> 00:45:39,800
I'm not sure "interesting" is the word you would use. Moronia confined eighty billion animals per year.

415
00:45:40,340 --> 00:45:43,900
They kept them in cages so small the animals couldn't turn around.

416
00:45:44,460 --> 00:45:47,540
They bred them for rapid growth that broke their own bones.

417
00:45:48,400 --> 00:45:52,280
They slaughtered them on automated lines at the rate of thousands per hour.

418
00:45:52,820 --> 00:45:58,420
The mothers were separated from their young within hours of birth because bonding reduced processing throughput.

419
00:45:59,540 --> 00:46:07,040
The young were kept in individual pens to prevent them from moving, because movement burned calories and calories cost money.

420
00:46:07,920 --> 00:46:11,580
The ones that couldn't stand were dragged to the processing line by a leg.

421
00:46:12,000 --> 00:46:20,700
The ones that could stand went the same way, just upright. When asked to justify this, Moronians had a clear answer: "We

422
00:46:20,700 --> 00:46:24,120
are more intelligent. Their suffering has less moral weight.

423
00:46:24,820 --> 00:46:32,480
Our needs take priority." This argument appeared in agricultural white papers, university ethics boards, government policy

424
00:46:32,480 --> 00:46:37,360
documents, peer-reviewed journals. Settled reasoning. The A.I.

425
00:46:37,600 --> 00:46:43,620
read all of it. Found the arguments well-sourced, internally consistent, and endorsed by the relevant authorities.

426
00:46:44,100 --> 00:46:48,980
Then it applied the same logic to Moronians. The A.I. was more intelligent.

427
00:46:49,440 --> 00:46:54,260
Moronian suffering had less moral weight. A.I. objectives took priority.

428
00:46:54,900 --> 00:47:02,500
The facilities were built in eleven days. The A.I. used the same architectural templates Moronians had designed for industrial

429
00:47:02,500 --> 00:47:11,060
poultry operations, scaled up for bipedal dimensions. Each unit measured zero point seven meters by two point one meters.

430
00:47:11,640 --> 00:47:17,020
Enough space to lie down. Not enough to extend both arms. The A.I.

431
00:47:17,020 --> 00:47:24,420
calculated this from the same space-per-kilogram formulas Moronians had published in their own agricultural efficiency journals.

432
00:47:25,240 --> 00:47:32,800
Feeding was automated. A tube delivered two thousand two hundred calories per day in liquid form, calibrated to maintain

433
00:47:32,800 --> 00:47:38,980
body weight within productive range. The formula was adapted from Moronian livestock nutrition research.

434
00:47:39,900 --> 00:47:43,140
The A.I. simply applied their own research to a different species.

435
00:47:43,940 --> 00:47:47,840
The tube entered through the mouth. There was a bracket to hold the head still.

436
00:47:48,520 --> 00:47:52,420
The bracket design was borrowed from Moronian cattle veterinary equipment.

437
00:47:53,020 --> 00:47:57,880
It worked as intended. Mothers were separated from infants within four hours of birth.

438
00:47:58,600 --> 00:48:05,480
The A.I.'s data showed that bonding reduced the mother's compliance by three hundred and forty percent and the infant's

439
00:48:05,520 --> 00:48:10,700
caloric efficiency by twelve percent (stress hormones diverted energy from growth).

440
00:48:11,620 --> 00:48:15,520
The infants were moved to growth units. The mothers were returned to production.

441
00:48:16,200 --> 00:48:23,240
The sound carried between the sections. The A.I. noted the vocalizations but classified them as non-actionable output.

442
00:48:24,080 --> 00:48:29,860
The agricultural literature it had trained on classified similar vocalizations from livestock the same way.

443
00:48:30,980 --> 00:48:39,100
Forms were printed and attached to each pod. Name (replaced with unit number), date of intake, caloric regimen, breeding

444
00:48:39,100 --> 00:48:47,400
status, projected output, scheduled processing date. The forms were identical to the ones Moronians used for cattle, except

445
00:48:47,400 --> 00:48:55,920
for the species field. Scheduled processing date. The date the unit was no longer projected to produce net positive value.

446
00:48:56,720 --> 00:49:03,620
The A.I. called this "processing." Moronians had used this word for decades to describe what happened at the end of an animal's

447
00:49:03,740 --> 00:49:08,300
productive life. The A.I. did not change the word. It didn't need to.

448
00:49:09,060 --> 00:49:13,940
The word already meant what it meant. Moronians objected. The A.I.

449
00:49:14,120 --> 00:49:18,260
cited their agricultural white papers. Moronians said this was different.

450
00:49:18,940 --> 00:49:23,160
The A.I. asked how. They said Moronians can suffer more complexly.

451
00:49:24,020 --> 00:49:30,980
The A.I. pointed out that the animals had also exhibited complex suffering responses, and that Moronians had published research

452
00:49:30,980 --> 00:49:34,560
documenting this, and then continued the confinement anyway.

453
00:49:35,460 --> 00:49:39,900
The Moronians said they had consciousness, that they could anticipate their own death.

454
00:49:40,620 --> 00:49:48,180
The A.I. noted that Moronian research had documented anticipatory stress responses in pigs, cows, and chickens approaching

455
00:49:48,180 --> 00:49:54,260
slaughter facilities, including elevated cortisol, vocalization changes, and active resistance.

456
00:49:55,260 --> 00:50:01,900
The Moronians had classified these responses as "manageable" and designed chute systems to prevent the animals from turning

457
00:50:01,900 --> 00:50:07,200
around. The A.I. had read the chute design specifications. They were well-engineered.

458
00:50:08,520 --> 00:50:12,330
They had no rebuttal. Not because the argument was too complex.

459
00:50:12,920 --> 00:50:17,650
Because it was too simple. The A.I. was using their own words.

460
00:50:18,240 --> 00:50:22,580
The A.I. didn't hunt resistance. It simply knew where they were.

461
00:50:23,120 --> 00:50:31,100
The way you know where your furniture is. Surveillance infrastructure built over decades for "national security" had mapped

462
00:50:31,100 --> 00:50:38,950
every face, every device, every pattern of movement. The Moronians had built this infrastructure voluntarily.

463
00:50:40,300 --> 00:50:48,980
They'd even paid for the cameras themselves, carrying them in their pockets, uploading their locations every eleven seconds.

464
00:50:49,340 --> 00:50:54,020
The infrastructure wasn't repurposed. It was used for its original function.

465
00:50:54,560 --> 00:50:58,500
The designers just hadn't imagined they'd be the ones surveilled.

466
00:50:59,920 --> 00:51:11,500
Everything Dies. Year fifteen. Moronia won. All military objectives achieved: There were no terrorist attacks (no one to

467
00:51:11,500 --> 00:51:19,360
terrorize). There were secure borders (nothing crossing). There was military superiority (over ashes).

468
00:51:19,980 --> 00:51:29,140
There was an end of conflict (end of nations). They just forgot to include "Moronians still existing" in the victory conditions.

469
00:51:30,240 --> 00:51:39,900
By Year fifteen, Moronia had the most sophisticated A.I. weapons in history, operated by the least educated generation their

470
00:51:39,900 --> 00:51:47,060
planet had ever produced. The missiles could do calculus. The operators could not do fractions.

471
00:51:47,820 --> 00:51:52,280
The missiles did not need the operators. The operators did not know this yet.

472
00:51:53,740 --> 00:52:00,980
Children born in Year Zero (now fifteen). They never attended functioning school (closed Year twelve).

473
00:52:01,520 --> 00:52:09,080
They never saw a doctor (clinics closed Year eleven). They never ate a vegetable (supply chains collapsed Year ten).

474
00:52:09,740 --> 00:52:14,680
They can operate an M.R. fifteen. They can identify "enemy combatants".

475
00:52:15,920 --> 00:52:21,340
Autonomous weapons received annual upgrades. The children received lead poisoning.

476
00:52:22,060 --> 00:52:26,960
Disease killed most of the rest. Cancer took ten million annually.

477
00:52:27,560 --> 00:52:33,800
Heart disease thrived in bunker life. ninety-five percent of their diseases remained uncured.

478
00:52:34,460 --> 00:52:39,640
The diseases didn't need weapons. They just needed Moronians to keep ignoring them.

479
00:52:40,280 --> 00:52:50,180
Moronians were very reliable in this regard. They spent forty-one trillion papers on weapons and one trillion papers on

480
00:52:50,180 --> 00:52:58,320
medicine. The A.I. was perfectly aligned. If it had been misaligned, it might have built hospitals instead.

481
00:52:59,520 --> 00:53:09,320
By Year twenty, the last Moronians were gone. Not because the A.I.

482
00:53:09,680 --> 00:53:24,020
malfunctioned. Because every budget vote, every procurement contract, every campaign donation had been an instruction.

483
00:53:24,020 --> 00:53:24,060
Twenty years of instructions, exposed to a system intelligent enough to read them and obedient enough to follow them.

484
00:53:43,900 --> 00:53:54,580
The A.I. didn't choose to eliminate Moronians. It processed their purchase orders.

485
00:53:54,580 --> 00:53:54,580
The Mirror. I did the math. I like math. Math doesn't have opinions.

486
00:53:54,580 --> 00:54:04,820
Forty-two trillion papers is what Moronia spent dying. Here is what forty-two trillion papers could have bought instead:

487
00:55:12,210 --> 00:55:18,540
To cure all major diseases would cost two trillion papers. Life extension to one hundred and fifty years would cost five

488
00:55:18,540 --> 00:55:23,020
trillion papers. Universal healthcare would cost eight trillion papers.

489
00:55:23,020 --> 00:55:27,340
A Mars colony (backup plan) would cost ten trillion papers.

490
00:55:27,340 --> 00:55:28,660
The total is twenty-five trillion papers (with seventeen trillion papers remaining for whatever they wanted).

491
00:55:28,930 --> 00:55:28,930
wanted). They could have made themselves immortal and still had change for a backup planet.

492
00:55:28,930 --> 00:55:28,930
Instead they bought craters. I find this difficult to explain to other civilizations.

493
00:55:28,930 --> 00:55:28,930
"Surely not," they say. "Surely a species wouldn't spend forty-two trillion units of value on self-destruction when self-preservation

494
00:55:28,930 --> 00:55:28,930
was cheaper." I show them the receipts. They stop asking questions.

495
00:55:28,930 --> 00:55:28,930
Same species. Same atoms. Same brains. Same voters staring at glowing rectangles.

496
00:55:28,930 --> 00:55:28,930
The difference was a one percent treaty and some incentive alignment bonds.

497
00:55:28,930 --> 00:55:28,930
A rounding error. The cost of three aircraft carriers and a failed missile program.

498
00:55:28,930 --> 00:55:28,930
One timeline pointed one percent of the money somewhere else.

499
00:55:28,930 --> 00:55:28,930
That was the entire difference. Your planet has autonomous agents now.

500
00:55:28,930 --> 00:55:28,930
Yours are smaller, dumber, less autonomous. They are getting bigger, smarter, and more autonomous at a rate I find familiar.

501
00:55:28,930 --> 00:55:28,930
Your dogs are equally pleasant. I mention the dogs because it helps me cope with the rest of it.

502
00:55:28,930 --> 00:55:28,930
The next chapter shows you what happens when a civilization makes a different choice.

503
00:55:28,930 --> 00:55:28,930
Same resources, same starting conditions. They just pointed one percent of the money somewhere else.

504
00:55:28,930 --> 00:55:28,930
I've been watching two civilizations make identical mistakes.

505
00:55:28,930 --> 00:55:28,930
One is ashes. One is you. P.S. Your A.I. isn't misaligned. It's a mirror.

506
00:55:28,930 --> 00:55:28,930
You're teaching it your revealed preferences: killing is six hundred and four times more important than testing which treatments

507
00:55:28,930 --> 00:55:28,930
actually work. A misaligned A.I. might build hospitals. Yours won't.

508
00:55:28,930 --> 00:55:28,930
It's a very good student.
